# Discriminated unions

A union discriminant (`kind`, `type`, `status`, …) is an **enum**, not an inline string-literal type.

In this repo that means a const-object enum (and `StringEnum` when the value is parsed from input). Do not introduce TypeScript `enum` syntax.

```typescript
// ✅
export const TenantActorKind = {
  user: 'user',
  apiKey: 'api_key',
} as const

export type TenantActorKind = (typeof TenantActorKind)[keyof typeof TenantActorKind]
export type UserActorKind = typeof TenantActorKind.user
export type ApiKeyActorKind = typeof TenantActorKind.apiKey

export type TenantActor =
  | {readonly kind: UserActorKind; readonly id: UserId}
  | {readonly kind: ApiKeyActorKind; readonly id: ApiKeyId}

if (actor.kind === TenantActorKind.user) {
  // actor.id is UserId
}

// ❌
export type TenantActorKind = 'user' | 'api_key'

export type TenantActor =
  | {readonly kind: 'user'; readonly id: UserId}
  | {readonly kind: 'api_key'; readonly id: ApiKeyId}

if (actor.kind === 'user') {
}
```

Construct and compare with the enum member (`TenantActorKind.user`), never a raw string.
